@page
@model CSharpMasteryHub.Pages.DevOps.WhatIsDevOpsModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "What is DevOps?";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-lg-10 mx-auto">

            <!-- Breadcrumb -->
            <nav aria-label="breadcrumb" class="mb-3">
                <ol class="breadcrumb">
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/DevOps">DevOps Hub</a></li>
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item active">What is DevOps?</li>
                </ol>
            </nav>

            <!-- Header -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <span class="badge bg-success mb-2">Beginner</span>
                <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold">What is DevOps?</h1>
                <p class="lead text-muted">
                    A complete beginner guide. Simple English. Real examples.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted small">
                    <i class="fas fa-clock me-1"></i> Reading time: 10 minutes
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: The Simple Truth -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">📖 What is DevOps? (Simple Words)</h3>
                    <p>
                        <strong>DevOps</strong> is a way of working. It means
                        <strong>Dev</strong>elopers (people who write code) and
                        <strong>Op</strong>erations (people who run servers) work
                        together as <strong>one team</strong>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In the old way, developers wrote code and then
                        <em>threw it over a wall</em> to another team. That team
                        put it on servers. If something broke, both teams blamed
                        each other.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        In DevOps, there is no wall. The same team builds the app,
                        tests it, puts it on servers, and keeps it running.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-primary border-0 bg-primary bg-opacity-10 mt-3">
                        <strong>💡 Think of it like this:</strong><br />
                        Imagine you bake a cake. In the old way, you bake it and
                        give it to someone else to deliver. In DevOps, you bake
                        it, package it, deliver it, and check the customer is
                        happy. You own the <strong>whole process</strong>.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 2: The Real Problem DevOps Solves -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">🤔 Why Does DevOps Exist?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Before DevOps, companies had big problems:
                    </p>

                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered">
                            <thead class="table-light">
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Problem</th>
                                    <th>What Happened</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Slow releases</strong></td>
                                    <td>Deploying new code took days or weeks.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Bugs found late</strong></td>
                                    <td>By the time users found bugs, fixing them was very expensive.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Blame games</strong></td>
                                    <td>When things broke, developers blamed operations and vice versa.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Manual work</strong></td>
                                    <td>People clicked buttons to deploy. They made mistakes.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>No monitoring</strong></td>
                                    <td>Companies did not know their app was slow until users complained.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p class="mt-3 mb-0">
                        DevOps fixes all of these problems with
                        <strong>automation</strong>,
                        <strong>communication</strong>, and
                        <strong>shared responsibility</strong>.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 3: The 5 Core Ideas of DevOps -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">🎯 The 5 Core Ideas of DevOps</h3>
                    <p>Every DevOps team follows these 5 ideas:</p>

                    <div class="row g-3">
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-users text-primary me-2"></i>1. One Team</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Developers and operations work together. No walls.
                                    Everyone is responsible for the whole product.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-robot text-success me-2"></i>2. Automate Everything</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Humans make mistakes. Machines do not. We automate
                                    build, test, and deploy.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-sync text-info me-2"></i>3. Small, Fast Changes</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Release small changes often. Small changes are
                                    easy to test and easy to fix if something goes wrong.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-6">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-chart-line text-warning me-2"></i>4. Always Measure</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Watch your system all the time. Know your speed,
                                    errors, and uptime. Fix problems before users notice.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-infinity text-danger me-2"></i>5. Keep Improving</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    After every release, ask: what can we do better?
                                    DevOps is a journey, not a destination.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 4: Real Client Example (Detailed) -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 border-start border-4 border-warning">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">🌍 Real Client Example: Sarah's Bakery</h3>

                    <p>
                        <strong>Client:</strong> Sarah owns a small bakery in Toronto.
                        She sells cakes and pastries.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Her problem:</strong> She takes orders by phone.
                        She writes them on paper. She loses orders and cannot grow.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Her goal:</strong> Build an online store where customers
                        can order cakes 24/7.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">🧑‍💻 How We Built It With DevOps</h5>

                    <div class="mb-3 p-3 bg-light rounded-3">
                        <strong>Step 1: Build the app</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">
                            We built a web app with ASP.NET Core and SQL Server.
                            It has a product list, shopping cart, and a checkout
                            with Stripe payment.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mb-3 p-3 bg-light rounded-3">
                        <strong>Step 2: Package with Docker</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">
                            We put the app inside a Docker container. Now it runs
                            the same on Sarah's laptop, our test server, and the
                            cloud. No "it works on my machine" problems.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mb-3 p-3 bg-light rounded-3">
                        <strong>Step 3: Automate with CI/CD</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">
                            We set up GitHub Actions. Every time we push code,
                            it automatically builds, tests, and deploys to Azure.
                            No manual work.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mb-3 p-3 bg-light rounded-3">
                        <strong>Step 4: Monitor with Prometheus</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">
                            We set up monitoring. If the site is slow or down,
                            we get an email immediately. We fix the problem
                            before Sarah even knows.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mb-0 p-3 bg-success bg-opacity-10 rounded-3 border border-success">
                        <strong>🎉 The Result</strong>
                        <ul class="mb-0 small mt-1">
                            <li>Sarah's Bakery has a real online store.</li>
                            <li>Orders went up 40% in the first month.</li>
                            <li>Deployments take 5 minutes, not 2 days.</li>
                            <li>The site stays online 99.9% of the time.</li>
                            <li>Sarah adds products herself. No developer needed.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 5: Key Tools -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">🛠️ Key Tools You Will Learn</h3>
                    <p>
                        These are the tools every DevOps engineer must know.
                        You will learn all of them in this course.
                    </p>
                    <div class="row g-3">
                        <div class="col-md-4">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fab fa-docker text-primary me-2"></i>Docker</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Packages your app so it runs the same everywhere.
                                    Stops "it works on my machine" problems.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-4">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fab fa-github text-dark me-2"></i>GitHub Actions</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Automates build, test, and deploy. Runs your
                                    pipeline every time you push code.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-4">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-dharmachakra text-primary me-2"></i>Kubernetes</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Manages many containers at once. Scales up
                                    when traffic is high. Restarts crashed apps.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-4">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-cloud text-info me-2"></i>Terraform</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Write code to create cloud servers. No
                                    clicking buttons. Repeatable and safe.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-4">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-chart-line text-success me-2"></i>Prometheus</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Collects numbers (CPU, memory, errors) from
                                    your app so you can see problems early.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-md-4">
                            <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded-3 h-100">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold"><i class="fas fa-layer-group text-warning me-2"></i>Grafana</h6>
                                <small class="text-muted">
                                    Shows your system data in beautiful dashboards.
                                    Easy to understand at a glance.
                                </small>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 6: DevOps vs Old Way -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">⚖️ DevOps vs The Old Way</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered">
                            <thead class="table-light">
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Task</th>
                                    <th>Old Way</th>
                                    <th>DevOps Way</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Deploy new code</td>
                                    <td>2 days (manual)</td>
                                    <td>5 minutes (automatic)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Find bugs</td>
                                    <td>After users complain</td>
                                    <td>Before deploy (auto-tests)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Who fixes problems?</td>
                                    <td>"Not my job"</td>
                                    <td>Everyone together</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Servers setup</td>
                                    <td>Click buttons in a portal</td>
                                    <td>Write code (Terraform)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Know if app is slow</td>
                                    <td>User tells you</td>
                                    <td>Alerts tell you first</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 7: A Day in the Life of a DevOps Engineer -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">🗓️ A Day in the Life of a DevOps Engineer</h3>
                    <p>Here is what a typical DevOps engineer does in a day:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Morning:</strong> Check dashboards. Look for any overnight problems.</li>
                        <li><strong>Mid-morning:</strong> Review pull requests. Help teammates fix build issues.</li>
                        <li><strong>Afternoon:</strong> Improve the CI/CD pipeline. Add a new automated test.</li>
                        <li><strong>Late afternoon:</strong> Update Terraform files to add a new test server.</li>
                        <li><strong>End of day:</strong> Deploy a new feature. Watch the metrics to make sure it is safe.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">
                        <i class="fas fa-info-circle me-1"></i>
                        Notice: A DevOps engineer does not just "run servers."
                        They write code, automate processes, and improve reliability.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 8: Summary -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 bg-primary bg-opacity-10">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">✅ What You Learned</h3>
                    <ul class="mb-0">
                        <li>DevOps means developers and operations work as <strong>one team</strong>.</li>
                        <li>It solves slow releases, late bugs, and blame games.</li>
                        <li>The 5 core ideas: <strong>One team, automate, small changes, measure, keep improving</strong>.</li>
                        <li>Key tools: Docker, GitHub Actions, Kubernetes, Terraform, Prometheus, Grafana.</li>
                        <li>A DevOps engineer writes code, automates pipelines, and monitors systems every day.</li>
                        <li>DevOps is not a job title — it is a <strong>way of working</strong>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 9: Test Yourself -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 border-start border-4 border-success">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">🧠 Test Yourself</h3>
                    <p>Answer these 5 questions in your head. Then check the answers below.</p>

                    <ol>
                        <li>What does the word <strong>DevOps</strong> mean?</li>
                        <li>Why was the "old way" slow and painful?</li>
                        <li>Name 3 tools a DevOps engineer uses.</li>
                        <li>In Sarah's Bakery example, how long did deployment take after DevOps?</li>
                        <li>What are the 5 core ideas of DevOps?</li>
                    </ol>

                    <button class="btn btn-success btn-sm mt-2" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#answersBox">
                        <i class="fas fa-eye me-1"></i>Show Answers
                    </button>

                    <div class="collapse mt-3" id="answersBox">
                        <div class="p-3 bg-success bg-opacity-10 rounded-3">
                            <ol class="mb-0">
                                <li><strong>DevOps</strong> = Developers + Operations working as one team.</li>
                                <li>The old way was slow because teams did not talk, deployments were manual, and bugs were found late.</li>
                                <li>Any 3 of: <strong>Docker, GitHub Actions, Kubernetes, Terraform, Prometheus, Grafana</strong>.</li>
                                <li>Deployment took <strong>5 minutes</strong> (instead of 2 days).</li>
                                <li>The 5 ideas: <strong>One team, automate everything, small fast changes, always measure, keep improving</strong>.</li>
                            </ol>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Navigation -->
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
                <a href="/DevOps" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
                    <i class="fas fa-arrow-left me-2"></i>Back to Hub
                </a>
                <a href="/DevOps/02-DockerBasics" class="btn btn-primary-custom">
                    Next: Docker Basics <i class="fas fa-arrow-right ms-2"></i>
                </a>
            </div>

        </div>
    </div>
</div>